bevy-horizon: let NAK's scheduler before register allocation wait out latencies (NAK_DEBUG=plat)

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at 606441c9, after 0001-0014. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies it; this text before the first
"diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

NAK's scheduler before register allocation (opt_instr_sched_prepass.rs, added in Mesa's
b55b8da0, "nak: Add a prepass instruction scheduler") places each block's instructions from the
bottom up. When it places one, it works out the earliest cycle at which each instruction feeding
it may go (the placed one's cycle plus the latency between them) and keeps that in instr_info, a
copy of the dependence graph's labels, so that the graph can be reused when a block is scheduled
again under another register target. But the instruction is then queued by
FutureReadyInstr::new, which reads the cycle from the graph's own labels, and this pass never
sets them. Every instruction is ready as soon as its uses are placed, the latencies go unused,
and the ready list's order (the longest path to the block's end, then the original order) walks
each chain of uses to its start. A texture read lands just above the instruction that uses it.
In Bevy's mesh preprocessing shader, eight depth reads feeding a tree of mins come out as two
reads, their min, the next two, and so on, so each pair waits for a round trip of its own.
The scheduler after register allocation sets the graph's labels itself and is unaffected.
Mesa's main branch had the same code on 11 October 2026.

With NAK_DEBUG=plat the queued instruction takes its cycle from instr_info. The dependence
graph, the register targets and the switch to scheduling for register pressure are unchanged;
only when an instruction may be placed changes, so what each shader computes doesn't. NAK_DEBUG
flags key NVK's shader cache already, so code compiled with and without the flag is cached
apart. Without the flag nothing changes.

On a PC through nakhost (findings 36), NAK_DEBUG=stats with the flag reports for Skate 3's
shaders: the eight preprocessing reads all issued before the first min; NAK's static cycle
estimate down 11-15% for the world's fragment shaders, 18-22% for the depth pyramid's and 21-22%
for mesh preprocessing; registers and warps per multiprocessor unchanged, but for the world's
vertex shader, 24 registers to 32 with its warps unchanged at 64. Whether the estimate holds on
the console's GPU is the question the flag is there to answer.

diff --git a/src/nouveau/compiler/nak/api.rs b/src/nouveau/compiler/nak/api.rs
index 23c7d16..506a1ef 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/compiler/nak/api.rs
+++ b/src/nouveau/compiler/nak/api.rs
@@ -36,6 +36,7 @@ enum DebugFlags {
     UBra,
     XBrk,
     LSink,
+    PLat,
 }
 
 pub struct Debug {
@@ -70,6 +71,7 @@ impl Debug {
                 "ubra" => flags |= 1 << DebugFlags::UBra as u8,
                 "xbrk" => flags |= 1 << DebugFlags::XBrk as u8,
                 "lsink" => flags |= 1 << DebugFlags::LSink as u8,
+                "plat" => flags |= 1 << DebugFlags::PLat as u8,
                 unk => eprintln!("Unknown NAK_DEBUG flag \"{}\"", unk),
             }
         }
@@ -139,6 +141,10 @@ pub trait GetDebugFlags {
     fn lsink(&self) -> bool {
         self.debug_flags() & (1 << DebugFlags::LSink as u8) != 0
     }
+
+    fn plat(&self) -> bool {
+        self.debug_flags() & (1 << DebugFlags::PLat as u8) != 0
+    }
 }
 
 pub static DEBUG: OnceLock<Debug> = OnceLock::new();
diff --git a/src/nouveau/compiler/nak/opt_instr_sched_prepass.rs b/src/nouveau/compiler/nak/opt_instr_sched_prepass.rs
index 0680e3e..02d29bb 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/compiler/nak/opt_instr_sched_prepass.rs
+++ b/src/nouveau/compiler/nak/opt_instr_sched_prepass.rs
@@ -579,8 +579,24 @@ impl<'a> GenerateOrder<'a> {
                 );
                 dep_instr.num_uses -= 1;
                 if dep_instr.num_uses <= 0 {
-                    future_ready_instrs
-                        .insert(FutureReadyInstr::new(g, edge.head_idx));
+                    // FutureReadyInstr::new reads the graph's label, which
+                    // this pass never updates (the graph is shared between
+                    // attempts), so the ready cycle above would go unused
+                    // and every instruction would be ready at once.
+                    let future_ready_instr = if DEBUG.plat() {
+                        let label = &g.nodes[edge.head_idx].label;
+                        FutureReadyInstr {
+                            ready_cycle: Reverse(
+                                dep_instr
+                                    .ready_cycle
+                                    .saturating_sub(label.exec_latency),
+                            ),
+                            index: edge.head_idx,
+                        }
+                    } else {
+                        FutureReadyInstr::new(g, edge.head_idx)
+                    };
+                    future_ready_instrs.insert(future_ready_instr);
                 }
             }
 
